    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.Scripts.Drivers.BasisLegSwivelDebug
    MemberName: BasisLegSwivelDebug
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 1
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Runtime diagnostic for the knee SWIVEL, per leg per frame. Built to
      answer one question: when one knee\n misbehaves and the other is fine, WHICH
      input differs? The solve path is mirror-symmetric (same\n kneeAnteriorRef,
      same captured bend normal, mirrored driver blocks, and an ODD anterior clamp),
      so a\n left/right asymmetry has to come from the DATA, and this is what reads
      the data.\n\n Rows are Basis.IK.BasisLegDiagnostics verbatim -- its own Header/ToRow,
      not a second copy\n of the field list, so the two cannot drift.\n\n The column
      that is NOT in the diagnostics struct is bendVsAnt: the angle between that
      leg's BendNormal\n (which rides the lower-leg TRACKER when FBIKTrackerBendNormal
      is on) and kneeAnteriorRef (which is always\n body-frame hips-right). Those
      two frames are what the anterior guard and the pole eases are measured\n against
      respectively, and the guard-first ordering in BasisLegSolveCore only keeps
      the near-colinear\n slerp well-conditioned while they agree to within ~90 deg
      -- measured 1.3x knee gain up to 90, then 316x\n at 120 and 471x at 180. A
      strap rotated on one shin, or a stale bend-normal capture, shows up here as
      a\n large bendVsAnt on that leg alone.\n\n Read the SUMMARY first; the CSV
      is for the frame the number goes bad."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
