  - TypeFullName: Basis.Network.Core.BasisCoreLease
    MemberName: BasisCoreLease
    Kind: Type
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 1
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "One subsystem's standing claim on the machine's cores.\n\n A subsystem
      does not decide its own width. It states three things it actually knows about\n
      itself \u2014 the floor below which it cannot function, the ceiling past which
      more cores stop\n helping it, and how busy it currently is \u2014 and reads
      back whatever the allocator granted.\n Nothing else in the server is allowed
      to size a pool against `ProcessorCount` directly,\n because every pool that
      does that is sizing against the whole machine while sharing it with\n the others.\n\n
      MaxCores is the load-bearing field and the one that is easy to get wrong. It
      is\n not \"how many cores would I like\"; it is \"how many can I convert into
      throughput\". Demand may\n move a lease anywhere inside its own [Min, Max];
      it can never argue its way past that\n ceiling. It is a delegate rather than
      a number because ceilings move at runtime \u2014 adding a\n send socket raises
      the send phase's immediately.\n\n But nobody actually knows that number. \"Eight
      workers per send socket\" is a real\n measurement, taken once, on one machine,
      at one player count \u2014 and the hosts this runs on\n range from a few container
      vCPUs to hundreds of weak cores, where the true figure is\n certainly something
      else. A declared ceiling is a hypothesis, so it is treated as one: it is\n
      the upper bound discovery starts from, and AddWork is how a lease earns the\n
      right to be measured instead of trusted. A lease that reports throughput has
      its real\n ceiling found by experiment (see BasisCpuBudget's discovery pass)
      and\n EffectiveMax drops to whatever that turns out to be. A lease that reports\n
      nothing keeps its declared number, because there is nothing to measure it against."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
