  - TypeFullName: Basis.Network.Core.
    MemberName: MergeHoldMs
    Kind: Field
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "How long (ms) a partly-filled merge buffer may wait for more data before
      being sent.\n\n The logic loop used to flush every peer's merge buffer on every
      pass, so at a few\n hundred passes a second most datagrams left less than half
      the MTU used and the server\n paid full per-packet cost for them. Holding a
      partial buffer briefly lets consecutive\n passes coalesce into one datagram.\n\n
      A full buffer is always sent immediately, so this only ever delays small sends,
      and it\n is a ceiling rather than an added delay \u2014 traffic that already
      fills the MTU is\n unaffected. 0 restores the old flush-every-pass behaviour.\n\n
      Measured at 500 players, same bytes on the wire throughout: 0 ms = 175K datagrams/s\n
      (~670 B each, under half the MTU), 3 ms = 147K (-16%), 8 ms = 96K (-45%, ~1125
      B each).\n 3 is the default because it is most of the packet-rate win for a
      delay well under one\n avatar send interval; raise it toward 8 if packet rate
      matters more than voice latency."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: Basis.Network.Core.
    MemberName: PeerUpdateParallelism
    Kind: Field
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Worker cap for the transport's per-peer update pass. 0 = a quarter
      of the cores,\n floored at 4 and capped at 8.\n\n This pass runs hundreds of
      times a second and does little work per peer, so letting it\n spread across
      every core costs far more in thread wake-up and GC-poll traffic than it\n saves.
      Profiling a 500-player server found 40 threads in this pass, three quarters
      of\n all GC-poll time coming from the parallel machinery itself rather than
      the work."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
