  - TypeFullName: LiteNetLib.
    MemberName: _peersPerUpdateWorker
    Kind: Field
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 0
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Peers per worker when PeerUpdateParallelism is auto.\n\n A fixed cap
      is wrong at one end or the other. Capping at 8 regardless of population\n fixed
      the oversubscription at 500 players but starved the pass at 3500, where a single\n
      pass was measured peaking at 1204 ms. That interval is the floor on reliable
      latency:\n the direct-connect handshake needs several round trips inside the
      client's 4 s budget,\n so P2P times out long before anything else looks wrong.
      Scaling with the peer count\n keeps a small instance cheap and a large one
      responsive."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
    Since: 
    ObsoleteMsg: 
    Platforms: []
  - TypeFullName: LiteNetLib.
    MemberName: PeerPassTargetMs
    Kind: Field
    ParamCount: 0
    ParamNames: []
    ParamDocs: []
    BaseTypeName: 
    Interfaces: []
    IsAbstract: 0
    IsSealed: 0
    IsStatic: 0
    IsPartial: 0
    IsMemberStatic: 0
    IsMemberReadOnly: 0
    IsMemberConst: 1
    IsMemberVirtual: 0
    IsMemberOverride: 0
    Summary: "Pass duration above which the pool is under-provisioned. Diagnostic
      only.\n\n Three controllers were built to size this pool from live load and
      all three measured\n worse. Duty cycle is degenerate \u2014 the loop only waits
      when a pass fits inside UpdateTime,\n so anything longer reports ~1.00 whether
      it takes 3 ms or 300 ms. Steering on pass\n duration alone cannot tell a host
      with spare cores, where widening is free, from a\n saturated one, where it
      took CPU from 16.2 to 25.2 cores at 2000 players and lowered\n throughput.
      Sizing stays explicit; this exists so an operator can see if theirs is right."
    Remarks: 
    Returns: 
    Value: 
    Examples: []
    TypeParamNames: []
    TypeParamDocs: []
    ExceptionCrefs: []
    ExceptionDocs: []
    SeeCrefs: []
    SeeAlsoCrefs: []
